WEBVTT

00:00:00.105 --> 00:00:02.789
A database serves many users at the same

00:00:02.789 --> 00:00:03.184
time.

00:00:03.618 --> 00:00:06.026
The concurrency control system has to run

00:00:06.026 --> 00:00:08.053
their transactions together, avoid

00:00:08.053 --> 00:00:10.355
anomalies, and still keep performance

00:00:10.368 --> 00:00:10.737
high.

00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:13.251
Why not just run one transaction at a

00:00:13.265 --> 00:00:13.686
time?

00:00:14.107 --> 00:00:16.580
Because the goal is to exploit parallelism

00:00:16.791 --> 00:00:18.659
and get more transactions per second.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:21.699
Serial execution can limit throughput,

00:00:22.225 --> 00:00:25.041
especially when transactions wait for I/O,

00:00:25.488 --> 00:00:27.765
as in banks and ticket reservations.

00:00:28.648 --> 00:00:30.305
Here is our running example.

00:00:30.727 --> 00:00:33.161
Two transactions update the same account

00:00:33.174 --> 00:00:35.069
D, which starts at 100.

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:37.898
One adds 3 and the other adds 6.

00:00:38.319 --> 00:00:40.148
If they run one after the other, the

00:00:40.148 --> 00:00:43.095
result is 109 in either order.

00:00:43.516 --> 00:00:45.398
This is the correct reference behaviour.

00:00:46.238 --> 00:00:49.028
Now let both transactions read before

00:00:49.422 --> 00:00:50.488
either one writes.

00:00:50.909 --> 00:00:51.962
Each starts from 100.

00:00:52.383 --> 00:00:57.541
T1 writes 103, then T2 writes 106, and the

00:00:57.554 --> 00:00:59.172
plus 3 vanishes.

00:00:59.633 --> 00:01:01.804
Think of two people editing the same

00:01:01.804 --> 00:01:04.146
document from the same old copy, where the

00:01:04.146 --> 00:01:05.343
last save wins.

00:01:06.172 --> 00:01:08.790
A dirty read happens when a transaction

00:01:08.948 --> 00:01:11.488
reads a value that was never committed.

00:01:11.909 --> 00:01:16.462
T1 writes 103, T2 reads it, and then T1

00:01:16.462 --> 00:01:17.238
rolls back.

00:01:17.659 --> 00:01:20.343
T2 has built its update on a value that

00:01:20.343 --> 00:01:24.238
never officially existed, so D ends at 109

00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:26.606
instead of the correct 106.

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.805
A non-repeatable read means you read the

00:01:29.805 --> 00:01:32.503
same item twice and get different answers.

00:01:32.937 --> 00:01:36.911
T1 reads D, T2 updates it in between, and

00:01:36.924 --> 00:01:39.661
when T1 reads again the two values differ.

00:01:40.575 --> 00:01:41.601
Phantoms are subtler.

00:01:41.996 --> 00:01:45.588
In a phantom update, the three values A, B

00:01:45.851 --> 00:01:48.246
and C must always sum to 100.

00:01:48.667 --> 00:01:51.786
T2 moves 10 from C to B, which is legal.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:56.351
But T1 read A and B before the move and C

00:01:56.351 --> 00:01:58.641
after it, so it sees a sum of 90.

00:01:59.062 --> 00:02:01.904
In a phantom insert, a new row appears

00:02:01.904 --> 00:02:04.470
that matches an earlier query of T1.

00:02:05.302 --> 00:02:07.934
To recap, there are five anomalies:

00:02:08.355 --> 00:02:11.381
lost update, dirty read, non-repeatable

00:02:11.381 --> 00:02:14.157
read, phantom update and phantom insert.

00:02:14.578 --> 00:02:17.131
In each one, another transaction

00:02:17.144 --> 00:02:19.434
interferes with what a transaction has

00:02:19.434 --> 00:02:21.210
read or is about to write.
